<chapter xml:id="ch-data-shapes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Data Shapes</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-data-shapes">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> worked because every
      dataset in it was already arranged the way that a good plotting system
      expects: one row per observation, one column per variable.
      Add a variable, add an aesthetic mapping.
      That trick only works if the data is already in the right
      <term>shape</term> for it.
      Real data rarely arrives that way, and the same numbers, rearranged, can
      be either trivial or nearly unusable depending on how they are laid out.
      This chapter is about recognising that shape is a real, consequential
      property of a dataset, not a formatting detail.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: The opener, on its own, told in full                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  TODO: expand on this / explain better what we mean by "data shapes"

   <section xml:id="sec-intro-data-shapes">
    <title>TODO</title>
  <p>
    <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission"/> described something that, looked 
    at again, is a shape problem. Sierra Leone's Ebola response did not 
    fail to merge its district data because anyone lost it or typed it 
    wrong. It failed because <q>each district response centre had adopted 
    its own method for recording cases,</q> and inconsistent Excel 
    line-list formats across districts <q>caused ongoing problems merging 
    and aggregating cases into national totals.</q> Different districts 
    were not disagreeing about what happened. They were recording the 
    same kind of information in incompatible shapes.
  </p>

  <p>
    One district might have one row per patient. Another might have one 
    row per day, with patient counts as columns. A third might have 
    separate sheets for confirmed and suspected cases. Each format made 
    sense locally — it matched the workflow of the people filling it in. 
    But when the national team tried to stack them together into a single 
    picture of the outbreak, the shapes did not fit. Cases could not be 
    counted without first deciding what a row meant, and that decision 
    was different in every district file.
  </p>

  <p>
    This chapter is about data shape: what it means, why it matters, and 
    the operations that change it. The three core operations are 
    <term>appending</term> — stacking datasets that have the same columns 
    but different rows; <term>merging</term> — joining datasets that share 
    a common identifier but carry different information; and 
    <term>reshaping</term> — converting data between wide formats (one 
    column per measurement type) and long formats (one row per 
    measurement). Any one of these would have helped the Ebola response. 
    In practice, real data problems usually require all three.
  </p>
</section>    

