<section xml:id="sec-data-shapes">
  <title>Data Shapes: Structuring, Binding, and Joining Tables</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Real-world data rarely arrives in a single, clean table ready for analysis. 
      Understanding data shapes means mastering how tables change layout,
      how rows are stacked, how variables are defined, and
      how primary keys connect separate datasets without corrupting information.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- 1. WIDE TO LONG                                                   -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ss-wide-to-long">
    <title>Wide to Long: When a Table's Shape Can't Hold Still</title>

    Add in our original story again back here as it has images/etc. (maybe not needed actually if 1. is done well. Let's see.)
    <p>
      In January 2020, researchers at Johns Hopkins University (JHU) launched
      a public COVID-19 dashboard to track global infections.
      The underlying dataset was published openly and laid out in what is
      called a <term>wide</term> shape: one row per country or region, and
      one column for every single calendar date, holding that place's
      running case count on that day.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most wide tables have a fixed, known set of columns that doesn't
      change.
      This one did not.
      Every single day the pandemic continued, the table needed one more
      column than it had the day before.
      Anyone who wanted to calculate a trend, work out a weekly rate, or
      merge in population figures first had to reshape the table into a
      different layout, called <term>long</term> shape: one row per country
      per date, with the date itself moved into its own column instead of
      being a column header.
      A wide table where every date is a different column header has no
      single, stable column that another dataset can be matched against; a
      long table does <mdash/> a fixed set of columns
      (<c>region</c>, <c>date</c>, <c>cases</c>) that behaves the same way
      no matter how many days have passed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every time analysts reshaped JHU's files into long format and matched
      them up with country codes for merging, an upstream change by JHU
      <mdash/> adding new columns, or reorganising how regions were labelled
      <mdash/> would break their reshaping scripts all over again.
      Wide and long are not a right shape and a wrong shape; wide is easy for
      a person to scan by eye on a single day, long is what a computer needs
      for tracking something over time or joining it to anything else.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is not a guess about how JHU's data behaved.
      The team that built and ran the dashboard published their own account
      of the difficulty, in a paper aptly titled <foreign>data collection
      process, challenges faced, and lessons learned</foreign>.
      Reporting standards changed as the pandemic went on, formats varied
      from one source feeding the dashboard to another, and every one of
      those changes rippled downstream into anyone else's code that had
      been written to expect a particular shape.
      The dashboard's own repository still carries a note for exactly this
      reason: the table's structure was adjusted more than once over the
      life of the project, and any script written against an earlier
      structure would need to be updated to keep working.
      A dataset that keeps changing shape underneath you is not a hypothetical
      teaching example; it was daily reality for one of the most-used
      datasets of the pandemic.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-wide-long-pivot">
      <title>Pivoting Standardizes Keys for Downstream Joins</title>

      <p>
        Wide tables store observation attributes across column headers; long
        tables store observation attributes as row values under a unified key
        column.
        Transforming wide data to long format is often a mandatory precursor
        to joining external demographic or temporal variables.
      </p>
    </insight>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- 2. DATA DEFINITIONS                                               -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ss-data-definitions">
    <title>Same Column Name, Different Definition</title>

    <p>
      A statistician might receive three datasets to combine: one from health
      facility logs, one from civil death registration, and one from a
      household survey.
      Each dataset contains a column named <c>maternal_death</c>, and in each,
      some rows are marked <c>TRUE</c>.
      The datasets look perfectly compatible.
      A software join executes without a single error.
      Yet a successful software merge does not make the three <c>TRUE</c>
      values the exact same claim.
    </p>

    <p>
      In 2014, Kenya worked to strengthen its Maternal Death Surveillance and
      Response (MDSR) system, as reported by Smith et al. (2017).
      At the time, official national estimates placed annual maternal deaths
      at approximately 8,000, derived from a modeled maternal mortality ratio
      of 510 deaths per 100,000 live births.
      Concurrently, the District Health Information System (DHIS2) recorded
      exactly 945 maternal deaths in health facilities.
    </p>

    <p>
      The eight-fold difference between 945 and 8,000 was not a calculation
      error, but a difference in operational definitions.
      The national estimate attempted to capture maternal deaths across the
      entire population (including deaths at home), while DHIS2 recorded only
      deaths occurring inside health facilities that were formally entered
      into logbooks.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-mmr-sources">
      <title>Maternal Mortality Observation Systems in Kenya</title>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Data Source</cell>
          <cell>Observation Scope &amp; Mechanism</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>DHIS2 Facility Logs</cell>
          <cell>
            Maternal deaths recorded directly inside health facility registers
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>MDSR Reviews</cell>
          <cell>
            Notified maternal deaths undergoing formal clinical audit reviews
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Civil Registration (CRVS)</cell>
          <cell>
            Officially issued death certificates with classified causes of death
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>DHS Household Surveys</cell>
          <cell>
            Retrospective sibling mortality reported by survey respondents
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>UN Model Estimates</cell>
          <cell>
            Statistical population modeling combining multiple administrative inputs
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Smith et al. (2017) found that of the 945 deaths recorded in DHIS2 in 2014, only 484
      (51.2%) underwent formal audit reviews.
      Of those audited, 447 (92.4%) were assessed as having received
      suboptimal care, and in 394 cases (81.4%), assessors judged that
      different management could have altered the outcome.
      Even after notification forms were integrated directly into DHIS2,
      structural gaps remained between DHIS2 and Civil Registration systems.
    </p>

    <p>
      This operational challenge is universal.
      A meta-analysis by Ahmed, Cresswell, and Say (2023) evaluated maternal
      death reporting globally and estimated that maternal death recording was
      34% incomplete, with overall maternal mortality underestimated by 39%
      due to underreporting and misclassification.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Data shapes</term> is not merely about managing rows and columns
      in code.
      It is about understanding what an observation and a variable actually
      represent in the physical world.
      Two tables that look identical in software schema can still measure
      fundamentally different realities.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-variable-definitions">
      <title>A Variable Name Is Not a Definition</title>

      Work on this one!

      <p>
        Software checks whether column names and data types match during a
        join; it cannot verify whether the underlying data-generation
        processes are comparable.
        Before combining data from different sources, ask not just
        <q>do the keys match?</q> but
        <q>are the things being combined actually measuring the same reality?</q>
      </p>
    </insight>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- 3. BINDING BY ROWS (APPENDING)                                    -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ss-binding-by-rows">
    <title>Binding by Rows: Stacking Farmer Lists for Fertilizer Vouchers</title>

    <p>
      Every planting season, the Kenyan government subsidises fertilizer for
      smallholder farmers and distributes it through National Cereals and
      Produce Board (NCPB) depots.
      A farmer registers once, and is issued a voucher by SMS: turn up at the
      depot with your phone and your ID, the clerk checks you against the
      system, and you walk away with fertilizer at a fraction of the market
      price.
      For a smallholder farmer, that price difference is not a minor saving.
      It can be the difference between planting a full acre and planting
      half of one.
    </p>

    <p>
      Behind that single lookup at the depot counter sits a chain of
      ordinary data work: registration lists have to be
      combined into one national list, and that list has to be checked
      against a farmer's ID and phone details before a voucher is issued.
    </p>

    <p>
      One way to build a national registry is to take every local list
      of registered farmers and stack them into a single table, one after
      another.
      In everyday data language this is called an <term>append</term>, or a
      <term>row bind</term>.
      For it to work cleanly, every list being stacked has to agree on its
      columns: the same column names, meaning the same thing, all the way
      down.
      If one office's spreadsheet calls a column <c>phone_number</c> and
      another calls the same thing <c>mobile_no</c>, software does not stop
      and ask what to do.
      It creates a new column for the name it hasn't seen before, and fills
      it with <term>NA</term> <mdash/> <q>not available</q>, the standard way
      software marks a hole in the data <mdash/> everywhere that column does
      not apply.
      A registry that should have five clean columns quietly grows extra
      ones nobody asked for, and a farmer's real phone number can end up
      split across two of them depending on which office registered them.
    </p>

    <p>
      A Kenyan phone number can correctly be written as <c>0712345678</c>,
      <c>254712345678</c>, or <c>+254 712 345 678</c> <mdash/> a person reads
      all three and knows immediately they are the same number.
      A join does not.
      To a join, <c>"0712345678"</c> and <c>"254712345678"</c> are two
      unrelated pieces of text.
      A join that cannot find a match does not fail loudly.
      It simply leaves that row without a match, quietly, as if the person
      had never registered at all.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-schema-drift">
      <title>Computers Match Characters, Not Intent</title>

      <p>
        Two records that look identical to a human being can be completely
        different to a computer if the column names, spacing, or exact text
        differ by even a single character.
        An append needs every table to agree on its columns before you stack
        them, and ideally needs a rule for what counts as
        <q>the same real-world entity twice</q> before it lets a duplicate
        in.
        A join needs a shared column to be written the exact same way in
        every table it touches.
        Neither operation will warn you when this goes wrong <mdash/> it will
        simply hand back a table with holes, or duplicates, in it, and leave
        you to notice.
      </p>
    </insight>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- 4. MERGING / INTRODUCING THE IDEA OF A KEY                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ss-merging-keys">
    <title>Merging and the Idea of a Key: The Homa Bay Health System</title>

    <p>
      Homa Bay County, on the shore of Lake Victoria, has a significant HIV
      burden and a well-established HIV care programme.
      Health facilities there run on electronic medical record systems, the
      same as facilities across much of the country, but a 2025
      study of Homa Bay's digital health landscape found that its single
      county referral hospital was running not one such system, but seven,
      side by side.
      Three of those seven, all built specifically for HIV care, do share a
      common patient identifier with each other.
      The other four <mdash/> covering general outpatient care, inpatient
      stays, the laboratory, and faith-based providers <mdash/> connect to
      none of the HIV systems, and not reliably to each other either.
    </p>

    <p>
      To make that concrete: picture a hypothetical patient, built from the
      study's own findings rather than a specific case it reported.
      A woman is HIV-positive and pregnant.
      Her HIV care sits in the HIV system.
      Her antenatal visits, being outside HIV care, are logged somewhere
      else.
      If she is admitted to hospital, her inpatient stay goes somewhere else
      again.
      This is not a stretch: the study's own interviews describe exactly
      this pattern in general, with people <q>assigned multiple numbers for
      different purposes, i.e., at the inpatient, outpatient and antenatal
      clinics.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Every operation involving merging tables depends on the same quiet assumption:
      that somewhere in your data, there is a column that reliably means
      <q>this same real-world thing</q>, everywhere it appears.
      That column is called a <term>key</term>.
      Homa Bay's hospital records show what happens when that assumption
      holds for only part of a system.
    </p>

    <p>
      The 2025 study found that patient identifiers were manually generated
      in most of the county's EMR systems and manually allocated at first
      interaction in 97% of facilities, with no county-wide identifier
      linking a person across every system they might touch.
      Within HIV care specifically, a shared identifier does exist and does
      its job.
      Step outside HIV care, and there is no column, anywhere in the
      hospital, that reliably means <q>this same person</q> across the rest
      of the systems she might touch.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is not a missing-data problem.
      Her details are captured correctly, every single time, in whichever
      system records them.
      The problem is that there is nothing to <term>join</term> or <term>merge</term> most of
      those correct records together on.
      Stack or link the non-HIV systems anyway, without a shared key, and
      one of two things happens.
      Either the same person is silently counted more than once across
      different systems, inflating whatever gets reported to the county or
      the ministry at the end of the month, or a clinician treating her for
      a complication during pregnancy has no way of seeing, from where they
      are sitting, that she is currently on HIV treatment at all <mdash/>
      even though that exact information exists, correctly recorded, one
      system over.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before you can join two tables, or even meaningfully ask whether they
      should be joined, something has to exist that means the same
      real-world thing in both of them.
      Sometimes the first job in a data project is not fixing a key.
      It is building one, from nothing, because nobody thought to build it
      in the first place.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-homa-bay-no-key">
      <title>You Cannot Join What Was Never Given a Shared Key</title>

      <p>
        A join, a stack, a reshape <mdash/> every one of these operations
        assumes a key already exists linking your tables together.
        When separate systems are built independently, with no agreement in
        advance on how a person, place, or thing will be identified across
        all of them, no amount of clever code recovers that link later.
        The key has to be designed in, before the data is collected, not
        discovered afterward.
      </p>
    </insight>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- 5. WHAT IF THE KEY IS WRONG?                                      -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ss-key-errors">
    <title>What If the Key Is Wrong? The IEBC Voter Register</title>

    <p>
      Before national elections in Kenya, the Independent Electoral and
      Boundaries Commission (IEBC) has to check its master voter register
      against other government records: identity records, to catch invalid
      or duplicate registrations, and death records, to remove voters who
      have since died.
      Checking one table against another like this is a <term>lookup</term>,
      or <term>join</term>, and the sensible key to join on is each person's
      National ID number.
      The same register also periodically needs its own duplicates removed,
      a step usually called <term>deduplication</term>: find rows that
      represent the same person entered twice, and collapse them into one.
    </p>

    <p>
      Both of these jobs depend entirely on one thing: having a key that is
      both correct for every row, and unique to one person.
      Get that wrong, and a register can break in two opposite directions
      at once.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>A key that isn't actually unique inflates the numbers.</term>
      If a system ever falls back to matching people on something other
      than a clean, unique identifier <mdash/> a full name and date of
      birth together, say <mdash/> that can go wrong in an easy-to-miss
      way.
      If ten voters share a name and date of birth, and a second table
      contains three other people with that same name and date of birth, a
      join does not pick one sensible match.
      It matches every one of the ten against every one of the three,
      producing thirty rows out of what should have been, at most, one real
      match.
      Run that same overly broad matching rule through a deduplication step
      instead, and the failure runs the other way: anyone who happens to
      share those same details with someone else gets treated as a
      duplicate of them, and is quietly deleted, even though they are two
      entirely different, entirely real people.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>A key that's untidy hides real matches.</term>
      Even with a clean, unique identifier as the key, small formatting
      differences break things: one system stores an ID as a plain number,
      another stores the same ID as text with a leading zero kept in front
      of it, a third has a stray space typed in at the end of the number by
      accident.
      To a human, these are obviously the same ID.
      To a join or a deduplication step, they are three unrelated pieces of
      text, and no match is ever found.
    </p>

    <p>
      What KPMG's 2022 audit of the IEBC register actually reported was a
      third variant of the same underlying problem: a shared, empty value.
      The audit found that <term>481,711</term> individuals were registered
      as voters with duplicated blank ID or passport reference fields
      <mdash/> records where that field had simply never been filled in.
      A blank matches a blank exactly as confidently as a real ID matches a
      real ID, so any process checking for duplicate identity references
      will flag every one of those empty-field records against every other
      one, whether or not the people behind them are actually the same.
      The same 2022 audit also found nearly 246,000 deceased voters still
      listed as active on the register, and 226,000 entries attached to
      invalid ID numbers.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-cartesian-explosion">
      <title>A Key Is Either Too Broad or Too Untidy, Rarely Neither</title>

      <p>
        A join should never make your row count bigger than expected; if it
        does, the key you joined on is not unique on at least one side.
        A deduplication step should only ever remove rows you can point to
        as genuine duplicates; if entries you know are distinct disappear,
        your key was too broad.
        And if a join or a deduplication misses matches that are obviously
        the same to a human eye, check spacing, leading zeros, and whether
        both sides are storing the value as the same kind of thing, before
        you trust the result.
      </p>
    </insight>
  </subsection>
</section>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-applied">
    <title>Wide and Long, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> has always been in <term>long</term> format:
        one row per bird, one column per variable.
        That shape is why every <c>ggplot2</c> call in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> worked without any
        reshaping.
        But long format is not always what you want to <em>look</em> at, and
        reshaping between the two is a skill in its own right.
      </p>

      <p>
        TODO: exercise idea?

idea?: Kenya's health and demographic data.

"Has vaccination coverage in Kenya improved, and does it vary by county?"

To answer that:

Survey data from multiple years (KDHS 2008, 2014, 2022) — each a separate file which need to be appended.
County-level population data from KNBS — a different source → you need to merge it
The vaccination data might come with one column per vaccine type (BCG, Polio, Measles...) — which is wide format → you need to pivot longer to plot it properly
And underneath all of this is the question of what a data rectangle actually is, and why the tidy principle of "one row per observation" matters

start with a question, discover the data is in three separate places in incompatible shapes, and work through each operation to get to an answer.

Introduction — the question (vaccination coverage by county over time), showing the three separate datasets that exist and why none of them alone answers it
Section 1: Appending — combining the three KDHS survey years into one dataset
Section 2: Merging — joining the population data to the survey data
Section 3: Longer/Wider — reshaping the vaccine columns so each vaccine is a row, enabling plotting
Section 4: Data Rectangles — now that we have done all three operations, step back and ask: what shape should data be in, and why?

Named definitions too?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-long-to-wide">
      <title>From long to wide: making a report table</title>

      <p>
        Suppose you want a simple summary table, birds per lake per species,
        laid out the way a person reading a report would expect: lakes down the
        side, species across the top.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)

        counts_long &lt;- flamingos %&gt;%
          count(lake, species)

        head(counts_long)

        #&gt;          lake          species  n
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria Greater Flamingo 10
        #&gt; 2     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo 114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo 30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita  Lesser Flamingo 38
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru Greater Flamingo 26
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo 126
      </program>
      <p>
        <c>counts_long</c> is exactly the shape a computer wants: one row per
        lake-species combination, one column holding the count.
        It is not the shape a human wants to read at a glance.
        <c>pivot_wider()</c> turns the <c>species</c> values into column names.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        counts_wide &lt;- counts_long %&gt;%
          pivot_wider(names_from = species, values_from = n)

        counts_wide

        #&gt;          lake Greater Flamingo Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria               10             114
        #&gt; 2 Elementaita               30              38
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru               26             126
      </program>
      <p>
        Same numbers, same information, a different shape <mdash/> and this is
        "table-lake-species-crosstab" from <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>,
        arrived at mechanically rather than typed by hand.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-wide-to-long">
      <title>From wide to long: getting back to something plottable</title>

      <p>
        Reverse the process with <c>pivot_longer()</c>, and <c>counts_wide</c>
        becomes plottable again.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        counts_wide %&gt;%
          pivot_longer(cols = -lake, names_to = "species", values_to = "n")

        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 3
        #&gt;   lake        species              n
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Bogoria     Greater Flamingo    10
        #&gt; 2 Bogoria     Lesser Flamingo    114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo    30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Lesser Flamingo     38
        #&gt; 5 Nakuru      Greater Flamingo    26
        #&gt; 6 Nakuru      Lesser Flamingo    126
      </program>
      <p>
        This round trip, long <mdash/> wide <mdash/> long, is not busywork.
        Each direction serves a different reader.
        Long format serves R: <c>ggplot2</c>, <c>group_by()</c>, and joins all
        expect it.
        Wide format serves a person scanning a table by eye, or a spreadsheet
        cell reference that assumes species live in fixed columns.
        Knowing which one a task actually needs, before writing any code, saves
        exactly the repeated reshaping that cost Gassen a day's work when JHU's
        columns shifted under him, and exactly the merging failure that cost
        Sierra Leone's Ebola response.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-data-shapes">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-jhu-daily">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-jhu-wide-problem"/> says a new column had to be added to
          the wide-format dataset every single day.
          Sketch, in words, what would have gone wrong for a downstream user's
          code if it referenced date columns by a fixed position (for example,
          <q>column 47</q> ) rather than by name.
          Would switching to long format on its own have fixed that particular
          problem?
          Why or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-ebola-standard">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-shapes-ebola-callback"/> argues that agreeing on a
          shape in advance is cheaper than reconciling incompatible shapes
          afterward.
          Propose one concrete rule Sierra Leone's district response centres
          could have agreed to in advance <mdash/> about column names, row
          structure, or file format <mdash/> that would have prevented the
          merging problem described in <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-flamingo-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="ss-shapes-long-to-wide"/> as a model, build a wide
          table of mean <c>body_mass_g</c> by <c>lake</c> (rows) and
          <c>species</c> (columns), instead of counts.
          Which version, this one or "table-lake-species-crosstab", would you
          send to someone who wanted to read it in an email, and which would you
          hand to someone who wanted to plot it in <c>ggplot2</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-gapminder-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>gapminder</c> package from
          <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> is in long format:
          one row per country per year.
          Using <c>pivot_wider()</c>, build a table with one row per country and
          one column per year, holding <c>lifeExp</c>.
          Try plotting directly from this wide version with the same
          <c>ggplot2</c> code from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d"/>.
          What breaks, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

